/** @page overview-continuous Continuous distributions

Continuous fits combine a transformed local-likelihood bulk estimator with an
optional finite-endpoint repair. The public `degree`, `bandwidth`, and
`multiplier` arguments retain their usual meaning for the bulk fit. Boundary
repair is enabled by default and can be disabled with `boundary_repair=false`.

@section continuous-bulk Bulk estimator and support transforms

With no finite support endpoint, data are fitted on their original scale. If
both endpoints @f$a<b@f$ are finite, the bulk fit uses a lightly regularized
probit transform. Writing @f$R=b-a@f$,

@f[
z(x)=\Phi^{-1}\left(\frac{x-a+5\times10^{-5}R}{1.0001R}\right).
@f]

If exactly one endpoint is finite, let @f$d(x)@f$ denote distance from it.
The scale @f$s@f$ is the weighted median of the observed distances, with a
maximum-distance and unit fallback for degenerate samples, and
@f$\epsilon=10^{-5}s@f$. The one-sided transform is

@f[
z(x)=4\left[\left\{\frac{\epsilon+d(x)}{s}\right\}^{1/4}
             -\left(\frac{\epsilon}{s}\right)^{1/4}\right],
\qquad
\left|\frac{dz}{dx}\right|
=\frac{1}{s}\left\{\frac{\epsilon+d(x)}{s}\right\}^{-3/4}.
@f]

The fourth-root transform and its Jacobian are scale equivariant. The small
offset keeps the transformed endpoint finite. Density values are transformed
back to the original scale and normalized on the interpolation grid.

An explicitly supplied `bandwidth` is interpreted on the transformed scale.
`get_bandwidth()` reports that bulk bandwidth before `multiplier` is applied;
it does not report an endpoint-expert bandwidth.

@section continuous-repair Boundary repair

`boundary_repair=true` makes each known finite endpoint eligible for repair;
it does not force an expert to be used. Repair is considered only for
continuous samples with at least 16 cleaned observations and effective sample
size

@f[
n_e=\frac{(\sum_i w_i)^2}{\sum_i w_i^2}
@f]

of at least 16. Each endpoint is then classified independently from ordered
original-scale distances. The classifier estimates the lower-tail power
@f$\beta@f$ from approximately @f$2\sqrt{n_e}@f$ effective observations. An
unweighted endpoint is repaired when

@f[
\widehat\beta\geq0.9,
\qquad
\widehat\beta\left(1-\frac{1.64485}{\sqrt{k}}\right)\leq1.
@f]

Weighted fits use effective counts and the more conservative lower threshold
0.975. Invalid, vanishing, exploding, or statistically ambiguous endpoint
behavior retains the bulk fit. Setting `boundary_repair=false` skips the
classifier and all expert calculations.

For a selected endpoint, the expert is the equal average of Gaussian
local-linear and local-quadratic equivalent-kernel estimates on the original
scale. Both use one automatically selected degree-two plug-in bandwidth. On
two-sided support the selector uses all positive-weight observations; on
one-sided support it uses the closest 75% by row count. Public case weights
enter the expert estimand and bandwidth selector. A manual bulk `bandwidth`
does not replace this expert bandwidth, while `multiplier` scales both.

The equivalent kernel is expressed as a pointwise combination of an ordinary
Gaussian KDE and its first two derivatives. These three convolutions are
evaluated on a 256-bin FFT grid. Invalid or nonpositive local-polynomial values
are set to zero before fusion.

@section continuous-fusion Fusion and normalization

Let @f$F_B@f$ be the normalized bulk CDF and

@f[
q=\min(1/4,n_e^{-1/2}),
\qquad
g(p)=1-3\min(1,p/q)^2+2\min(1,p/q)^3.
@f]

For selected lower and upper endpoints, the fusion weights are
@f$w_L(x)=g\{F_B(x)\}@f$ and @f$w_U(x)=g\{1-F_B(x)\}@f$; a missing or rejected
endpoint has weight zero. The final density before numerical normalization is

@f[
\widehat f(x)=w_L(x)\widehat f_L(x)
+\{1-w_L(x)-w_U(x)\}\widehat f_B(x)
+w_U(x)\widehat f_U(x).
@f]

Because @f$q\leq1/4@f$, the lower and upper regions do not overlap. The fused
values are clamped at zero and the existing interpolation-grid normalization
constructs a proper density.

@section continuous-edf Effective degrees of freedom

The reported EDF extends the existing conditional influence-trace
approximation. Bulk and expert diagonal influence numerators are fused with
the same weights as their densities, and their ratio gives the local
influence. The trace conditions on selected bandwidths, endpoint
classifications, CDF-based fusion weights, clamp states, and normalization;
derivatives of those adaptive choices are intentionally omitted. Pure-bulk
fits therefore retain the previous EDF calculation exactly.

*/
